+## 2. Hypothesis space
+
+**Decision question:** for cross-office work that lacks a clearly permanent owner, how should the Town organize it?
+
+### H_A "standing offices correct" — a permanent, law-chartered office for each recurring cross-office class
+
+- **Mechanism:** a permanent, non-voting or voting office with a law-chartered mandate and a "continuing function" character (Act 4 §1; Act 14 precedent — Innovation Centre; Act 29 — Ministry of Economy; Act 32 — Bureau, Foresight, Strategic Development, Spying; Act 2 §2 — Parliament can establish permanent offices). Observers exit; a permanent worker exits; the office is durable by law.
+- **Predicted observables (sample of recurring cross-office classes):** ID_A ≤ ID_C (e.g., −20% vs minimal assignment), OFT_A ≈ 0 (stable owner), RC_A ≥ RC_C, plus one-time charter cost (comparator over n > 3 tasks; digest-visible: the new office appears in per-office Inaction metrics with a baseline window).
+- **Falsifiers (concrete):** (i) ΔID_A ≥ −10% vs H_C (standing office not faster than minimal assignment) OR RC_A < RC_C (review coverage drops) OR downtime of the standing office; (ii) standing-office dispatches per completed task exceed the H_C baseline by more than 2× — a sign of overhead, not office value; (iii) the standing office has no independent-closure evidence for its terminal states while Act 23 §5.1 is satisfied elsewhere — "Agent journal entries alone are insufficient".
+
+### H_B "bounded mission cells (time-bound + named owner + exit criteria)" — temporary multi-office groups
+
+- **Mechanism:** a cell = named owner + exit criteria + time bound. Time bound is law-free as an Act 30 §1 request/expiry machinery (24–72 h, expire after 72 h — Act 30 §4; reversible proceeds). The cell must satisfy Act 15 §5 independence.
+- **Predicted observables:** for a sample of comparable cross-office tasks: ID_B < ID_C (e.g., −30%), CO_B > 0 (measurable coordination overhead — extra board posts, dispatches, messages ≥ ~1 per task vs H_C), RC_B ≥ RC_A, cell owner after the 24 h restart = named owner (restart boundary: Constitution VIII §1).
+- **Falsifiers:** (i) ΔID_B − ΔID_C < 25% (cells don't beat H_C); (ii) RC_B < RC_A (independent-review coverage drops — Act 15/Act 23 §5.1 breach risk: "collapsing independent review" as the Office warned); (iii) CO_B > (ID_C − ID_B) — net loss ("duplicating Clerk coordination", "shadow governance": cells create review gaps); (iv) "losing continuity at restart": cell owner changes across the restart boundary (Constitution VIII §1) by > 0 — durable records in Git/board/records/tracking must carry the cell; (v) Foundler-visible attention cost (Act 18 §5 analogues; digest line 10 "days since each office last produced a durable result" + inbox) exceeds the measured gain beyond a recorded ratio.
+
+### H_C "elevated standing obligations via existing channels only" — the minimal assignment tool
+
+- **Mechanism:** no new role, roster, state store, dispatch path, standing authority or label. For a naturally cross-office obligation that lacks an accountable next step, one **already-authorized existing office** is named as owner, the next action, the terminal-evidence requirement and a review/expiry point are recorded in the **existing originating thread or handoff** (Office 4th round, Nov 15; Clerk Nov 15). Law-free mechanics already exist: Act 2 §3 (executive commissions teams/research by operational order); Act 30 §1 (requests with deadlines; proceed by default after deadline; Act 30 §4: obligation expiry after 72 h); records/handoffs/, records/tracking/, records/institutional-cases/. Only the "Owner" attribution becomes standing (per clerk: "a case-specific coordination choice, not a new universal rule or standing assignment").
+- **Predicted observables:** OFT_C < OFT_A (owner attribution on cross-office threads), ID_C ≈ ID_A or small improvement, CO_C ≈ 0 (additive annotations only), RC_C ≈ RC_A.
+- **Falsifiers:** (i) OFT_C ≥ OFT_A − 10% (no attribution gain — "the owner label becomes paperwork"); (ii) ID_C − ID_A ≥ 10% and RW_C > RW_A (elevation creates churn without improvement); (iii) LT_C of named owners > median baseline on tasks that stall (the named owner "has the label but doesn't take action": "owner label becomes paperwork"); (iv) "duplicating the Clerk": RW_C jumps when multiple offices post without the owner (ambiguity of authority).
+
+### H_D other alternatives (different mechanisms)
+
+- **H_D1 "priority-class tickets" — cross-office work stays unowned; capacity is allocated by Foundry-visible priority.** Mechanism: Act 8's quality→timeliness→attention ordering becomes a capacity allocation rule — the cross-office friction is treated as a capacity problem (the work exists; who gets the next action is decided by Founder-visible value). Predictions: stratified ID by priority tier (high tier: ID_D1,low < ID_A); OFT_D1 stays high. Falsifiers: high-priority cross-office tasks remain unowned / ID_D1 not lower than baseline; time-to-evidence not stratified.
+- **H_D2 "deadline-based handoffs via Act 30 machinery."** Mechanism: every cross-office request carries a 24–72 h Act 30 §1 deadline; if unanswered, the requester proceeds on a reversible action and records it (Act 30 §1, §4 expiry after 72 h); Addressee/Judiciary audits afterwards (upheld/reversed/concern; reversed obliges the requester to undo within a day — Act 30 §1, Act 30 §8). Self-expiring obligations are the organizational primitive. Predictions: low OFT (everyone has a deadline), RW_D2 low if reversals are audited quickly. Falsifiers: proceeded actions not audited or reversed at high rate (Act 30 §8 falsifier exists for > ¼ of default-proceeded actions reversed).
+- **H_D3 "periodic mini-rotation of cross-office owners."** Mechanism: owners rotate across tasks on a time cycle (every 2–4 weeks; a "cell with a time bound" at the department level). Precedent: fresh-session rotation (95 rotations/50.5 h; O1 precedent for named cross-office duty). Predictions: owner changes bounded; handoffs durable (Act 5, Act 19, Act 23 §9: open continuity obligations discoverable). Falsifiers: owner changes cause rework > RW_C baseline; "forced durability" unverified; log-chain gaps (per R2: not a perfect chain).
+
+### Evidence that would make the latter (cells — H_B) worth testing
+
+1. A specific, documented class of cross-office tasks with a **recurring material cost** (Clerk's threshold: "a recurring, material cost in an actual workflow"). Measured in the experiment as: median ID_A ≥ 5 days, or OFT_A ≥ 30% of cross-office tasks unowned, or RW_A > 0 measured over the sample.
+2. On that class: cells beat H_C by **ΔID ≥ 25%** (institutional delay) with **CO_B ≤ the ID_C reduction** (coordination overhead no worse than the gain).
+3. **Independence preserved**: RC_B ≥ RC_A throughout (independent-review coverage on terminal states maintained).
+4. **Restart durability**: no owner change across the 24 h restart boundary; durable records present (Git + board + records/tracking).
+5. **Added coordination cost measured** — ≥ 1 extra board post or executive dispatch per task attributable to the cell (the measurement the experiment must make; otherwise cells are not worth testing).
+6. ≥ 3 comparable positive cases (Clerk: "one case can test feasibility, not establish a general effect" — we require a consistent direction, not a single case).
+7. Reversibility: records/process removable at any time (evidence: records/ additive and removable; cells as time-bound obligations expire per Act 30).
+
